add(
  'What is the one-way ANOVA model and its assumptions?',
  'The ONE-WAY ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE (one-way ANOVA) compares the means of k >= 2 independent groups. The MODEL is Y_{ij} = mu + alpha_i + epsilon_{ij}, for i = 1, ..., k (groups/treatments) and j = 1, ..., n_i (observations per group). Here mu is the GRAND MEAN, alpha_i is the TREATMENT EFFECT of group i (with sum_i n_i alpha_i = 0 as the identifiability constraint, or sum_i alpha_i = 0 in the balanced case n_i = n), and epsilon_{ij} ~ N(0, sigma^2) iid are the random errors. Equivalently Y_{ij} ~ N(mu_i, sigma^2) with mu_i = mu + alpha_i, and the HYPOTHESES are H_0: mu_1 = mu_2 = ... = mu_k (all group means equal) vs H_a: at least one mu_i differs. The CLASSICAL ASSUMPTIONS ( mnemonic "LINE"): (1) LINEARITY/ADDITIVITY — the model Y_{ij} = mu + alpha_i + epsilon_{ij} correctly specifies the mean structure; (2) INDEPENDENCE — observations are independent within and across groups (typically ensured by RANDOMIZATION and independent sampling; violated by repeated measures or clustered designs); (3) NORMALITY — the residuals epsilon_{ij} are normally distributed (assessed via Q-Q plot of residuals, Shapiro-Wilk test on residuals; robust for large n by CLT); (4) EQUAL VARIANCES (homoscedasticity) — Var(epsilon_{ij}) = sigma^2 is constant across all groups (assessed via residual-vs-fitted plot, Levene/Bartlett/Brown-Forsythe tests; rule of thumb: largest group SD <= 2 * smallest group SD). The TOTAL sample size is N = sum n_i. The DESIGN is BALANCED if all n_i are equal (n_i = n); UNBALANCED otherwise. The F-test is fairly ROBUST to non-normality (especially with larger n and balanced design) and to mild heteroscedasticity, but sensitive to violations of independence (the most critical assumption). When assumptions fail: use Welch ANOVA (heteroscedasticity), Kruskal-Wallis (non-normality), or generalized linear mixed models.',
  'ch08p10_oneway_anova_model',
  'formula_recall',
  ['one-way ANOVA', 'treatment effect', 'grand mean', 'normality', 'homoscedasticity', 'independence', 'LINE']
)
add(
  'How is the ANOVA decomposition derived for one-way ANOVA?',
  'In one-way ANOVA, the TOTAL SUM OF SQUARES is partitioned into BETWEEN-GROUP (treatment) and WITHIN-GROUP (error) components: SST = SSTR + SSE, where SST = sum_{i=1}^{k} sum_{j=1}^{n_i} (Y_{ij} - Ybar..)^2 (total, with N - 1 degrees of freedom, N = sum n_i), SSTR = sum_{i=1}^{k} n_i (Ybar_i. - Ybar..)^2 (treatment/between, with k - 1 df), and SSE = sum_{i=1}^{k} sum_{j=1}^{n_i} (Y_{ij} - Ybar_i.)^2 (error/within, with N - k df). Here Ybar_i. = (1/n_i) sum_j Y_{ij} is the GROUP MEAN and Ybar.. = (1/N) sum_{i,j} Y_{ij} is the GRAND MEAN. DERIVATION: write Y_{ij} - Ybar.. = (Ybar_i. - Ybar..) + (Y_{ij} - Ybar_i.), square both sides and sum over i, j. The cross-product term is 2 sum_i sum_j (Ybar_i. - Ybar..)(Y_{ij} - Ybar_i.) = 2 sum_i (Ybar_i. - Ybar..) sum_j (Y_{ij} - Ybar_i.) = 2 sum_i (Ybar_i. - Ybar..) * 0 = 0, since sum_j (Y_{ij} - Ybar_i.) = 0 by definition of the group mean. Hence SST = sum_i n_i (Ybar_i. - Ybar..)^2 + sum_i sum_j (Y_{ij} - Ybar_i.)^2 = SSTR + SSE. MEAN SQUARES: MSTR = SSTR / (k - 1) and MSE = SSE / (N - k). Under H_0 (all mu_i equal): E[MSTR] = E[MSE] = sigma^2; under H_a: E[MSTR] = sigma^2 + sum n_i alpha_i^2 / (k - 1) > sigma^2 = E[MSE]. So large values of MSTR / MSE indicate H_a. Under normality and H_0, SSE/sigma^2 ~ chi^2_{N-k} and SSTR/sigma^2 ~ chi^2_{k-1}, independent (Cochran\'s theorem). The F-STATISTIC is F = MSTR / MSE, distributed as F_{k-1, N-k} under H_0. The ANOVA TABLE has rows: Treatments (SSTR, k-1, MSTR, F), Error (SSE, N-k, MSE), Total (SST, N-1).',
  'ch08p10_oneway_anova_decomposition',
  'formula_recall',
  ['ANOVA decomposition', 'SST', 'SSTR', 'SSE', 'sum of squares', 'degrees of freedom', 'Cochran theorem']
)
add(
  'What is the F-test in one-way ANOVA and how is the p-value computed?',
  'In one-way ANOVA, the F-TEST evaluates H_0: mu_1 = ... = mu_k against H_a: at least one mean differs. The TEST STATISTIC is F = MSTR / MSE = [SSTR / (k - 1)] / [SSE / (N - k)], where SSTR = sum n_i (Ybar_i. - Ybar..)^2, SSE = sum sum (Y_{ij} - Ybar_i.)^2, k = number of groups, N = total sample size. Under H_0 (with normality, independence, homoscedasticity), F ~ F_{k-1, N-k} (Snedecor\'s F distribution with numerator df = k - 1 and denominator df = N - k). The F distribution is the ratio of two independent chi-squared variables each divided by its df: if U ~ chi^2_{d1} and V ~ chi^2_{d2} independent, then (U/d1)/(V/d2) ~ F_{d1, d2}. The test is UPPER-TAILED (one-sided): we reject H_0 when F > F_{k-1, N-k, 1-alpha} (large F means between-group variation exceeds within-group variation). The P-VALUE is p = P(F_{k-1, N-k} >= F_obs) = upper-tail probability, computed via the incomplete beta function: p = I_{d2/(d2 + d1 * F_obs)}(d2/2, d1/2). DECISION RULE: reject H_0 at significance level alpha if p < alpha (equivalently F_obs > F_{k-1, N-k, 1-alpha}). INTUITION: F compares "explained variance" (between groups) to "unexplained variance" (within groups). F near 1 -> no evidence against H_0 (group means indistinguishable relative to noise). F large -> at least one group mean differs from the others. IMPORTANT: rejecting H_0 only tells us that NOT ALL means are equal — it does NOT identify WHICH pairs differ. POST-HOC / multiple comparison procedures (Tukey HSD, Bonferroni, Scheffe, Dunnett, Duncan, Fisher LSD, Newman-Keuls, Games-Howell) are needed to identify specific differences while controlling the family-wise error rate or false discovery rate. POWER of the F-test depends on alpha, sample sizes n_i, the true effect size (e.g., Cohen\'s f or the noncentrality parameter lambda = sum n_i alpha_i^2 / sigma^2), and the degrees of freedom; computed via the noncentral F distribution F_{k-1, N-k}(lambda).',
  'ch08p10_oneway_anova_ftest',
  'formula_recall',
  ['F-test', 'p-value', 'F distribution', 'Snedecor', 'noncentral F', 'post-hoc', 'family-wise error']
)
add(
  'What are contrasts and orthogonal contrasts in one-way ANOVA?',
  'A CONTRAST is a LINEAR COMBINATION of group means with coefficients summing to zero: psi = sum_{i=1}^{k} c_i mu_i, where sum_i c_i = 0. The ESTIMATOR is psi_hat = sum_i c_i Ybar_i., with Var(psi_hat) = sigma^2 sum_i (c_i^2 / n_i) estimated by SE(psi_hat) = sqrt(MSE * sum_i c_i^2 / n_i). The t-statistic for H_0: psi = 0 is T = psi_hat / SE(psi_hat) ~ t_{N-k}, giving CI: psi_hat +/- t_{N-k, 1-alpha/2} * SE(psi_hat). TYPES of contrasts: (1) PAIRWISE — c_i = 1 for group A, c_j = -1 for group B, others 0 (tests mu_A - mu_B); (2) TREATMENT VS CONTROL — Dunnett: c_1 = -1 (control), c_i = 1/(k-1) for treatments (Dunnett-adjusted); (3) LINEAR TREND — c_i = i - (k+1)/2 (tests for linear trend across ordered groups); (4) QUADRATIC/CUBIC — orthogonal polynomial coefficients; (5) HELMERT — each level vs mean of subsequent levels; (6) DEVIATION — each level vs grand mean (with one constrained). ORTHOGONAL CONTRASTS: two contrasts psi_1 = sum c_i mu_i and psi_2 = sum d_i mu_i are ORTHOGONAL (independent under normality) iff sum_i c_i d_i = 0 (balanced) or sum_i (c_i d_i / n_i) = 0 (unbalanced). A set of k - 1 mutually orthogonal contrasts PARTITIONS the treatment sum of squares SSTR into k - 1 independent single-df components: SSTR = sum_{m=1}^{k-1} SSM_m, where SSM_m = psi_hat_m^2 / (sum_i c_{m,i}^2 / n_i). Each component is tested with its own F_m = SSM_m / MSE ~ F_{1, N-k} = t^2_{N-k}. ORTHOGONALITY BENEFITS: (a) independent tests (no inflation if each tested at alpha); (b) clean partition of variability; (c) natural for trend analysis (linear, quadratic, cubic components). CAUTION: orthogonality is a mathematical convenience, not a scientific necessity — planned (a priori) contrasts should reflect the research questions, orthogonal or not. PLANNED CONTRASTS (few, specified before seeing data) tested at level alpha are more powerful than post-hoc tests; POST-HOC contrasts (data-suggested, many) need multiplicity correction (Bonferroni, Scheffe, Tukey).',
  'ch08p10_oneway_contrasts',
  'formula_recall',
  ['contrast', 'orthogonal contrasts', 'linear combination', 'trend analysis', 'Helmert', 'Dunnett', 'planned contrasts']
)
add(
  'What are the Tukey HSD, Bonferroni, and Scheffe multiple comparison procedures?',
  'After a significant one-way ANOVA F-test, MULTIPLE COMPARISON PROCEDURES (MCPs) identify WHICH group means differ while controlling the FAMILY-WISE ERROR RATE (FWER) = P(at least one Type I error among all comparisons). The three classic MCPs: (1) TUKEY\'S HONESTLY SIGNIFICANT DIFFERENCE (HSD) — for ALL PAIRWISE comparisons (k(k-1)/2 pairs). Test statistic: q = (Ybar_i. - Ybar_j.) / sqrt(MSE/n) (balanced, n_i = n) where q is the STUDENTIZED RANGE distribution with parameters k and N-k. Reject H_0: mu_i = mu_j if |Ybar_i. - Ybar_j.| > q_{k, N-k, 1-alpha} * sqrt(MSE/n). For unbalanced designs, use the TUKEY-KRAMER adjustment: SE_{ij} = sqrt((MSE/2)(1/n_i + 1/n_j)). Tukey controls FWER EXACTLY for all pairwise comparisons under balanced design; it is MORE powerful than Bonferroni/Scheffe for pairwise. (2) BONFERRONI — general purpose for ANY set of m comparisons. Adjust: reject comparison i if p_i < alpha/m, equivalently use per-comparison alpha/m or CI with t_{N-k, 1-alpha/(2m)}. SIMPLE but CONSERVATIVE (actual FWER <= alpha, often much less); powerful for FEW planned comparisons, loses power as m grows. For all pairwise m = k(k-1)/2. (3) SCHEFFE — for ALL POSSIBLE contrasts (not just pairwise). Test statistic: F = psi_hat^2 / [MSE * (sum c_i^2 / n_i) * (k-1)] ; reject if F > F_{k-1, N-k, 1-alpha} (note the extra (k-1) factor). Equivalently CI: psi_hat +/- sqrt((k-1) F_{k-1, N-k, 1-alpha}) * SE(psi_hat). Scheffe controls FWER for the INFINITE family of all contrasts; MOST conservative for pairwise, but ONLY valid option for arbitrary post-hoc contrasts. COMPARISON: For pairwise — Tukey most powerful, then Bonferroni (if m small), then Scheffe. For few planned contrasts — Bonferroni. For complex post-hoc contrasts — Scheffe. OTHER MCPs: Dunnett (vs control), Games-Howell (unequal variances), Gabriel, REGW-Q/F, Holm (step-down Bonferroni, more powerful), Benjamini-Hochberg (controls FDR not FWER).',
  'ch08p10_oneway_multiple_comparisons',
  'formula_recall',
  ['Tukey HSD', 'Bonferroni', 'Scheffe', 'family-wise error', 'studentized range', 'Tukey-Kramer', 'Dunnett', 'Games-Howell']
)
add(
  'How is the power and sample size for one-way ANOVA computed?',
  'The POWER of the one-way ANOVA F-test is the probability of correctly rejecting H_0: mu_1 = ... = mu_k when H_a is true. Under H_a (specific nonzero alpha_i), the F-statistic follows a NONCENTRAL F distribution: F ~ F_{k-1, N-k}(lambda), where lambda = sum_{i=1}^{k} n_i alpha_i^2 / sigma^2 is the NONCENTRALITY PARAMETER (alpha_i = mu_i - mu, the treatment effects). The POWER is pi = P(F > F_{k-1, N-k, 1-alpha} | H_a) = 1 - F_{k-1, N-k, lambda}(F_{crit}), computed via the noncentral F CDF. EFFECT SIZE measures (standardize the magnitude of group differences, independent of units): (1) COHEN\'S f: f = sqrt(sum_i (n_i/N) (mu_i - mu)^2 / sigma^2) = sqrt(lambda / N); conventions: f = 0.1 small, 0.25 medium, 0.4 large; (2) ETA-SQUARED (population): eta^2 = SSTR / SST = lambda / (lambda + N); (3) OMEGA-SQUARED (less biased): omega^2 = (SSTR - (k-1) MSE) / (SST + MSE); (4) Cohen\'s f and eta^2 are related: f^2 = eta^2 / (1 - eta^2). SAMPLE SIZE determination (for fixed k, effect size f, alpha, desired power 1 - beta): iterate over N (or n per group in balanced design) computing lambda = N f^2 and power = 1 - F_{k-1, N-k, lambda}(F_{crit}) until power >= 1 - beta. RULES OF THUMB (Cohen): for f = 0.1 small, ~322 per group (k=3, alpha=0.05, power=0.80); f = 0.25 medium, ~52 per group; f = 0.4 large, ~21 per group. FACTORS AFFECTING POWER: (a) larger N -> higher power; (b) larger effect size -> higher power; (c) larger alpha -> higher power (but more Type I); (d) balanced designs maximize power for fixed N; (e) fewer groups k (for fixed N) -> more df for error -> slightly higher power. NONCENTRALITY alternative: use the APPROXIMATION by Patnaik (1950) — replace noncentral chi^2 with scaled central chi^2 — for hand calculation; modern software uses exact noncentral F. For UNEQUAL group sizes, the most efficient allocation (maximizing power for a specific contrast) puts more observations where the contrast coefficients are largest; for detecting ANY difference, BALANCED is optimal.',
  'ch08p10_oneway_power_sample_size',
  'formula_recall',
  ['power', 'sample size', 'noncentral F', 'Cohen f', 'effect size', 'eta-squared', 'omega-squared', 'noncentrality']
)
add(
  'What is Welch\'s ANOVA and when is it used for unequal variances?',
  'WELCH\'S ANOVA is a GENERALIZATION of one-way ANOVA for the case of UNEQUAL VARIANCES across groups (violation of homoscedasticity). The standard F-test assumes sigma^2 is constant; when group variances sigma_i^2 differ substantially (e.g., largest SD > 2 * smallest, or significant Levene/Bartlett test), the standard F-test has INFLATED Type I error. Welch\'s approach uses a WEIGHTED analysis with weights w_i = n_i / s_i^2 (inverse-variance weights, where s_i^2 is the sample variance of group i). Define the WEIGHTED MEAN wbar = sum w_i Ybar_i. / sum w_i, the BETWEEN-GROUP statistic D = sum_i w_i (Ybar_i. - wbar)^2 / (k - 1), and the WITHIN statistic V = [1 + 2(k-2)/(k^2-1) sum_i (1 - w_i/sum w_j)^2 / (n_i - 1)]. The Welch F-statistic is F_W = D / V, approximately distributed as F_{nu1, nu2} under H_0, with degrees of freedom: nu1 = k - 1 and nu2 = (k^2 - 1) / [3 sum_i (1 - w_i/sum w_j)^2 / (n_i - 1)] (Satterthwaite approximation, fractional df). DECISION: reject H_0 if F_W > F_{nu1, nu2, 1-alpha}. INTUITION: Welch weights groups by precision (more observations, less variance => more weight), and corrects the df downward to account for the extra uncertainty from estimating unequal variances. WHEN TO USE: (a) Levene or Bartlett test significant (heteroscedasticity present); (b) group SDs differ by factor > 2; (c) unbalanced design with unequal variances (the standard F-test is especially vulnerable here); (d) non-normal data with unequal spreads. PROPERTIES: (1) controls Type I error well even under extreme variance heterogeneity and unbalanced designs (unlike standard F which is liberal with large-variance small-n groups); (2) slightly less powerful than standard F when variances ARE equal (small loss); (3) robust to non-normality with moderate sample sizes. POST-HOC after Welch: use GAMES-HOWELL test (generalizes Tukey with Welch-style df correction) for pairwise comparisons; do NOT use Tukey HSD (which assumes equal variances). ALTERNATIVES to Welch: Brown-Forsythe (uses trimmed means and deviations from medians), robust ANOVA with bootstrap/permutation, generalized Welch with trimmed means (Yuen). The standard recommendation: ALWAYS report Welch\'s ANOVA alongside or instead of the standard F when variances are unequal.',
  'ch08p10_oneway_welch_anova',
  'formula_recall',
  ['Welch ANOVA', 'unequal variances', 'heteroscedasticity', 'Satterthwaite', 'Games-Howell', 'inverse-variance weights']
)
add(
  'What are the assumptions diagnostics and remedies in one-way ANOVA?',
  'The four ONE-WAY ANOVA assumptions (LINE: Linearity/Additivity, Independence, Normality, Equal variances) must be checked via DIAGNOSTICS, with REMEDIES applied when violations are detected. (1) INDEPENDENCE (most critical, cannot be "fixed" post-hoc): assessed by STUDY DESIGN — random sampling, randomization of treatment assignment, no clustering/repeated measures. If violated (e.g., repeated measures, clustered data): use REPEATED-MEASURES ANOVA, LINEAR MIXED MODELS with random effects, or GEE. Independence violations cause SEVERE inflation of Type I error. (2) NORMALITY of residuals: assessed via (a) Q-Q PLOT of residuals (visual: points should follow the 45-degree line); (b) HISTOGRAM of residuals (should be bell-shaped); (c) SHAPIRO-WILK test on residuals (H_0: normal, reject if p < 0.05); (d) Anderson-Darling test. Robust for large n (CLT: n_i >= 30 per group is usually adequate). REMEDIES: (i) transform Y (log for right-skewed/positive data, square root for counts, Box-Cox for general); (ii) nonparametric KRUSKAL-WALLIS test (rank-based, no normality); (iii) PERMUTATION F-test (exact, no distributional assumption); (iv) bootstrap. (3) EQUAL VARIANCES (homoscedasticity): assessed via (a) RESIDUAL-VS-FITTED plot (look for fan-shape/spreading); (b) LEVENE\'S test (H_0: equal variances, robust to non-normality; uses absolute deviations from group means/medians — Brown-Forsythe variant uses medians, more robust); (c) BARTLETT\'s test (sensitive to non-normality); (d) rule of thumb: max SD / min SD <= 2. REMEDIES: (i) WELCH\'S ANOVA (unequal variances F-test with Satterthwaite df); (ii) transform Y (log often stabilizes variance); (iii) weighted least squares; (iv) Games-Howell post-hoc. (4) ADDITIVITY/OUTLIERS: assessed via (a) residual plot patterns (curvature suggests non-additivity); (b) Cook\'s distance, leverage, studentized residuals for outliers; (c) Tukey\'s test for non-additivity. REMEDIES: transform, remove outliers (with justification), or use robust regression. GENERAL STRATEGY: (a) always plot data (boxplots per group, residual plots); (b) check assumptions in order of importance (independence >> equal variances > normality > additivity); (c) prefer robust alternatives (Welch, Kruskal-Wallis) if unsure; (d) report diagnostics transparently. The F-test is ROBUST to mild non-normality (especially balanced, large n) and mild heteroscedasticity (balanced), but NOT robust to independence violations.',
  'ch08p10_oneway_assumptions_diagnostics',
  'formula_recall',
  ['assumptions', 'diagnostics', 'Levene test', 'Bartlett test', 'Shapiro-Wilk', 'Q-Q plot', 'remedies', 'Box-Cox']
)

